    "message": {
     "content": "### The Verdict: **Negotiate**\n\nThis is an **above-average initial offer** for a Google L6 Product Manager, but **you should definitely negotiate**. Because Google reached out to you passively while you are well-positioned at Meta (IC6), you hold maximum leverage. Accepting the first offer would leave significant money on the table\u2014especially given the lack of a sign-on bonus and Google\u2019s front-loaded vesting schedule.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Offer Breakdown & Real Value\n\n#### A. How the Compensation Numbers Actually Work\nGoogle uses a **front-loaded vesting schedule** for initial equity grants (typically **33% / 33% / 22% / 12%** over 4 years):\n\n| Component | Year 1 | Year 2 | Year 3 | Year 4 |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $235,000 | $235,000 | $235,000 | $235,000 |\n| **Target Bonus (20%)** | $47,000 | $47,000 | $47,000 | $47,000 |\n| **Equity Vest (33/33/22/12)** | $356,400 (33%) | $356,400 (33%) | $237,600 (22%) | $129,600 (12%) |\n| **Total Comp (Pre-Refresh)** | **~$638,400** | **~$638,400** | **~$519,600** | **~$411,600** |\n\n* **4-Year Straight-Line Average (No growth/refresh):** **$552,000/year**\n* **Market Benchmark:** The median Google L6 PM total compensation in the Bay Area sits around **$480,000\u2013$530,000/year**, with top-of-band / competitive packages reaching **$600,000\u2013$680,000+** annualized.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Strengths and Weaknesses of the Offer\n\n#### What\u2019s Good:\n* **Initial Equity Grant ($1.08M):** At $270k/year average ($356k in Y1/Y2), this is already near the 65th\u201370th percentile for standard L6 initial grants.\n* **Level Mapping:** Meta IC6 to Google L6 is an accurate, lateral level match.\n\n#### What Needs Work:\n1. **No Sign-On Bonus ($0):** Google frequently uses sign-on bonuses to offset unvested equity or unvested annual bonus cycles when poaching senior talent from Meta. At L6, sign-on bonuses often land between **$50,000 and $100,000+**.\n2. **Base Salary Headroom:** Your offer is $235,000 against a posted range max of $271,000. There is room to push base to the $250,000\u2013$255,000 mark.\n3. **The Year 3\u20134 Equity Cliff:** Because initial grants drop from 33% down to 22% and 12%, you will be heavily dependent on annual refresher grants (which vest 25%/year) to maintain your Year 1/2 total comp. Meta's PM refresher culture is known to be among the most lucrative in big tech, making Google's unguaranteed refreshers an area of financial risk.\n\n---\n\n### 3. What Exactly to Ask For\n\nWhen negotiating with Google, focus on **Sign-on Bonus**, **Equity**, and **Base Salary** in that order of flexibility:\n\n| Component | Current Offer | Target Counter | Rationale |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Sign-on Bonus** | $0 | **$75,000 \u2013 $100,000** | Offsets leaving Meta's upcoming bonus/vesting cycle and bridges Year 1 cash. |\n| **RSU Grant (4-Yr)** | $1,080,000 | **$1,200,000 \u2013 $1,250,000** | Offsets Meta's equity refresh trajectory and mitigates Google's Year 3/4 drop. |\n| **Base Salary** | $235,000 | **$250,000 \u2013 $255,000** | Pushes toward the upper half of their posted band ($183k\u2013$271k). |\n\n---\n\n### 4. How to Frame the Negotiation (Script)\n\nSince you are happy at Meta and were passively recruited, you have the ideal negotiating posture: polite, enthusiastic about the team, but firm that the financial delta must justify walking away from your current equity and trajectory.\n\n#### Suggested Email / Talking Points for Your Recruiter:\n\n> *\"Thank you again for the offer. I've really enjoyed meeting the team and am excited about the scope of the role.*\n> \n> *As you know, I wasn\u2019t actively looking when Google reached out, and I\u2019m in a very strong spot at Meta\u2014both in terms of scope as an IC6 and my existing unvested equity/refresher trajectory. For me to walk away from my current trajectory and make the transition seamless, I'm looking for the package to be structured as follows:*\n> \n> 1. ***Sign-on Bonus:*** *$85,000 to offset the forfeited bonus and equity cycles at Meta.*\n> 2. ***Equity:*** *$1,200,000 in GSUs over 4 years to bridge the drop-off in years 3 and 4 relative to Meta's stacked refresher model.*\n> 3. ***Base Salary:*** *$250,000 to better align with the senior end of your posted SF band.*\n> \n> *If we can get to these numbers, I\u2019d be thrilled to sign immediately and decline my remaining internal projects at Meta.\"*\n\n---\n\n### 5. Summary Recommendation\n\n* **Do not accept as-is:** You have no risk of the offer being rescinded by professionally countering.\n* **Do not walk away yet:** Google's compensation committee frequently adjusts initial offers for strong passive Meta candidates when presented with clear counter-proposals.\n* **Bottom line:** Push for a **$75k\u2013$100k sign-on bonus** and a **~$1.2M equity grant**. Even if they meet you halfway (e.g., $50k sign-on + $1.15M equity), your Year 1 total compensation will cross **$680,000+**, making the transition financially worthwhile.",
